  "id": "U11",
  "article_id": "gnews:d4c5dc540392f8df39aee250",
  "title": "Nvidia Delays Next-Generation Kyber Server Architecture as Manufacturing Hurdles Give Rivals a Rare Opening",
  "publication_day": "2026-07-06",
  "article_opening": "Price change for Nvidia stock in the last 6 months: 4%\n$NVDA Share Price as of Jul. 2: $195\n52-Week High: $237\n$NVDA Stock Price Target: $302\nNvidia ( NVDA ) stock barely moved on Monday after news broke that one of the company’s biggest upcoming products is running late. Research firm SemiAnalysis reported that Kyber, Nvidia’s next rack-scale server design, has been pushed back more than a year , from 2027 to 2028.\nKyber matters because it’s built to pack 144 of Nvidia’s most powerful chips into a single cabinet, letting them work together like one giant computer. That kind of processing powe",
  "passage": "…arch firm SemiAnalysis reported that Kyber, Nvidia’s next rack-scale server design, has been pushed back more than a year , from 2027 to 2028.\nKyber matters because it’s built to pack 144 of Nvidia’s most powerful chips into a single cabinet, letting them work together like one giant computer. That kind of processing power is what AI companies need to train and run their most advanced models.\nThe design was supposed to launch alongside Vera Rubin Ultra, Nvidia’s next-generation system, in 2027. The holdup comes down to manufacturing problems with a specialized circuit board inside the system, according to SemiAnalysis.\nA larger version, called NVL576, which links eight racks together via optical connections, is also likely to be delayed or to ship only in small volumes.\nNvidia had a backup plan: bolting two current-generation racks together to achieve similar power, but that idea was scrapped too.\nCloud customers reportedly rejected it because the design was awkward and the service was expensive to run. SemiAnalysis says this leaves Nvidia without a proven way to scale up Rubin Ultra for now, which could give…",
  "statement": "The holdup comes down to manufacturing problems with a specialized circuit board inside the system, according to SemiAnalysis."
 },
 {
  "id": "U12",
  "article_id": "gnews:4d1677af37befd53ca3e7b45",
  "title": "NVIDIA is about to start taking a share of cloud providers' revenue.",
  "publication_day": "2026-07-01",
  "article_opening": "It has been reported that, in an effort to break the monopoly of tech giants, NVIDIA is providing financial guarantees for emerging cloud providers by offering GPU computing capacity lease-back arrangements to address their financing challenges. In exchange, NVIDIA will take a revenue share from these cloud providers, directly participating in profit distribution in the downstream computing market and extending its control further down the industrial chain.\nNVIDIA is leveraging its strong balance sheet to gain market leverage, quietly evolving from a chip vendor into the 'central bank' of the ",
  "passage": "… NVIDIA published a statement on its official website announcing a new business model that combines revenue sharing with credit support, enabling AI cloud providers to procure NVIDIA infrastructure without bearing the full upfront capital expenditure, and allowing them to offer computing services to downstream AI-native enterprises, model developers, and corporate clients.\nAccording to the report, the initiative is internally referred to by some NVIDIA personnel as the 'AI Compute Partnership.' A NVIDIA spokesperson also confirmed the existence of the program. This move marks a significant strategic shift for NVIDIA:\nOn one hand, it lowers financing barriers for emerging cloud service providers to expand its customer base; on the other, it directly participates in profit distribution within the downstream computing market through revenue sharing, thereby extending its control further down the AI value chain.\nBusiness Model Shift: From Selling Chips to Sharing Cloud Revenue\nAccording to NVIDIA’s official press release, the company will earn not only s…",
  "statement": "A NVIDIA spokesperson also confirmed the existence of the program."
 },
 {
  "id": "U13",
  "article_id": "gnews:6ab3379f10dfeb28924ebb57",
  "title": "NVIDIA DSX Promises More Revenue per Gigawatt. Who Actually Captures It?",
  "publication_day": "2026-07-06",
  "article_opening": "NVIDIA’s DSX reference design and operating system are engineered to raise the revenue per gigawatt an AI factory can produce by lifting tokens per megawatt. Paired with NVIDIA’s July 1 revenue-sharing model, DSX forms half of a two-part business model: grow the revenue a gigawatt generates, then collect a recurring cut of it. Sharon AI and Firmus are building the first DSX-aligned factories under the arrangement.\nHow NVIDIA’s DSX reference design and operating system raise revenue per gigawatt by lifting token efficiency\nThe DSX stack: reference designs, DSX Sim digital twin, the open source ",
  "passage": "…aching $100 billion per gigawatt make tokens-per-megawatt efficiency the decisive variable\nThe bull case of a compounding efficiency-plus-financing flywheel against the bear case of product-market mismatch and an unresolved split of the upside.\nThe News: On July 1, 2026, NVIDIA introduced a business model that lets AI cloud operators deploy large-scale, multi-tenant AI factories through a combination of revenue sharing and credit support, with the resulting facilities built as DSX AI factories. Participating clouds procure NVIDIA infrastructure and sell NVIDIA-powered services; NVIDIA earns its standard product revenue plus a share of the cloud revenue generated on the supported capacity. Sharon AI, deploying up to 40,000 Grace Blackwell GB300 GPUs across new capacity in Australia, and Firmus, building a 360-megawatt DSX-aligned AI factory campus in Batam, Indonesia, targeting up to 170,000 GPUs, are among the first partners.\nAnalyst Take: NVIDIA’s revenue-sharing model answered how the company gets paid twice on the same silicon. The DSX reference design and operating system is NVIDIA’s mechanism for raising the revenue per gigawatt that a fixed block of power can produce, and …",
  "statement": "Participating clouds procure NVIDIA infrastructure and sell NVIDIA-powered services; NVIDIA earns its standard product revenue plus a share of the cloud revenue generated on the supported capacity."
 },
 {
  "id": "U14",
  "article_id": "gnews:25d0b1d2a9dfe0bb99432e1a",
  "title": "NVIDIA Falls 7% in a Month: Should You Still Hold NVDA Stock or Exit?",
  "publication_day": "2026-07-06",
  "article_opening": "/ NVIDIA Falls 7% in a Month: Should You Still Hold NVDA Stock or Exit?\nNVIDIA Falls 7% in a Month: Should You Still Hold NVDA Stock or Exit?\nNVIDIA Corporation NVDA shares have declined 6.6% over the past month, underperforming the broader Zacks Computer and Technology sector, which slipped 2.2% during the same period. At first glance, the decline may look worrying, but a closer look suggests the weakness is more about market sentiment than company-specific problems.\nThe sell-off has been widespread across the semiconductor industry. Shares of Marvell Technology, Inc. MRVL, Broadcom Inc. AVGO",
  "passage": "…tforms are witnessing strong customer adoption, driven by growing demand for high-performance and energy-efficient AI computing. At the same time, NVIDIA is expanding beyond GPUs with networking products such as InfiniBand, Spectrum-X Ethernet and NVLink, allowing it to capture a larger share of AI infrastructure spending.\nThe numbers highlight this strength. During the first quarter of fiscal 2027, the Data Center segment generated $75.25 billion in revenues, accounting for 92% of total sales. Revenues from the segment surged 92% year over year and grew 21% sequentially, driven by the rapid ramp-up of Blackwell 300 systems and robust networking demand.\nAnother major advantage is NVIDIA's software ecosystem. Platforms like CUDA and its growing portfolio of AI software make it difficult for customers to switch to competing solutions. This creates a powerful competitive moat that extends well beyond hardware.\nAs AI adoption shifts from model training toward large-scale inference and enterprise deployment, NVIDIA appears well-positioned to benefit across multiple stages of the AI investment cycle.\nNVIDIA’s Financial Performance Remains Exceptiona…",
  "statement": "Revenues from the segment surged 92% year over year and grew 21% sequentially, driven by the rapid ramp-up of Blackwell 300 systems and robust networking demand."
 },
 {
  "id": "U15",
  "article_id": "gnews:795ea94e1100415a67fd1a96",
  "title": "Nvidia's Kyber NVL144 reportedly pushed back more than a year, Asian suppliers drop",
  "publication_day": "2026-07-06",
  "article_opening": "Nvidia's Kyber NVL144 reportedly pushed back more than a year, Asian suppliers drop\nA report from analyst firm SemiAnalysis says Nvidia's next-generation AI server rack has been delayed by more than a year. Suppliers across Japan, Taiwan, South Korea, and Hong Kong saw their stock prices drop sharply in response.\nAsian tech stocks slid after analyst firm SemiAnalysis reported on X that Nvidia's next AI server rack system, Kyber NVL144, has been pushed back more than twelve months to 2028 because of manufacturing problems.\nThe issue, according to SemiAnalysis, is the circuit boards. Specificall",
  "passage": "…edly pushed back more than a year, Asian suppliers drop\nA report from analyst firm SemiAnalysis says Nvidia's next-generation AI server rack has been delayed by more than a year. Suppliers across Japan, Taiwan, South Korea, and Hong Kong saw their stock prices drop sharply in response.\nAsian tech stocks slid after analyst firm SemiAnalysis reported on X that Nvidia's next AI server rack system, Kyber NVL144, has been pushed back more than twelve months to 2028 because of manufacturing problems.\nThe issue, according to SemiAnalysis, is the circuit boards. Specifically, the PCB midplane, a central board that connects all the individual components, has proven extremely difficult to produce without defects. Nvidia CEO Jensen Huang had shown off Kyber NVL144 just three months earlier at the company's GTC conference.\nThe report hit an already jittery investor base. After years of AI-fueled gains, even small setbacks trigger sharp sell-offs. Japanese PCB maker Ibiden, which counts Nvidia as its largest customer, dropped as much as ten percent. Kingboard Laminates fell 18 percent in Hong Kong, Elite Material lost ten percent in Taiwan, and Samsung Electro-Mechanics slid eleven percent in South Korea. …",
  "statement": "The issue, according to SemiAnalysis, is the circuit boards. Specifically, the PCB midplane, a central board that connects all the individual components, has proven extremely difficult to produce without defects."
 },
 {
