  "article_id": "gnews:3854aeddaa3f31de00cf2816",
  "title": "Nvidia offers to take a cut of AI cloud revenue on top of hardware sales in new optional financing vehicle — trades tokens for revenue cut",
  "publication_day": "2026-07-02",
  "article_opening": "Nvidia offers to take a cut of AI cloud revenue on top of hardware sales in new optional financing vehicle — trades tokens for revenue cut\nBy Luke James\nSharon AI and Firmus are the first partners, with a combined total of up to 210,000 GPUs.\nNvidia has announced a new business model under which it’ll be able to double-dip for revenue on the same silicon: once when its partner AI clouds use its hardware, and again as an ongoing percentage of the revenue that hardware generates. In a blog post co-authored by CFO Colette Kress, the company pitched the “revenue-sharing and credit-support model” a",
  "passage": "Nvidia offers to take a cut of AI cloud revenue on top of hardware sales in new optional financing vehicle — trades tokens for revenue cut\nBy Luke James\nSharon AI and Firmus are the first partners, with a combined total of up to 210,000 GPUs.\nNvidia has announced a new business model under which it’ll be able to double-dip for revenue on the same silicon: once when its partner AI clouds use its hardware, and again as an ongoing percentage of the revenue that hardware generates. In a blog post co-authored by CFO Colette Kress, the company pitched the “revenue-sharing and credit-support model” as a way to open compute access to startups that can’t finance it themselves. In practice, cash-poor AI companies trade a slice of whatever the…",
  "statement": "Sharon AI and Firmus are the first partners, with a combined total of up to 210,000 GPUs."
 },
 {
  "id": "U17",
  "article_id": "gnews:1e7a9c953f3ad70093b67ae4",
  "title": "Nvidia introduces revenue-sharing model for AI cloud financing",
  "publication_day": "2026-07-04",
  "article_opening": "Nvidia introduces revenue-sharing model for AI cloud financing\nNvidia has launched a revenue-sharing financing model aimed at AI cloud providers, facilitating access to its computing infrastructure. This initiative is designed to support startups and enterprises in scaling AI operations.\nNvidia launches a revenue-sharing financing model for AI cloud providers.\nThe model aims to facilitate access to large-scale AI compute for startups and enterprises.\nSharon AI and Firmus are early adopters of the financing structure.\nThe initiative reflects a shift in the AI market towards production inference",
  "passage": "…inancing model aimed at AI cloud providers, facilitating access to its computing infrastructure. This initiative is designed to support startups and enterprises in scaling AI operations.\nNvidia launches a revenue-sharing financing model for AI cloud providers.\nThe model aims to facilitate access to large-scale AI compute for startups and enterprises.\nSharon AI and Firmus are early adopters of the financing structure.\nThe initiative reflects a shift in the AI market towards production inference.\nNvidia has unveiled a revenue-sharing financing model to assist AI cloud providers in acquiring its computing infrastructure, thereby enhancing access to large-scale AI compute for startups, enterprises, researchers, and regional providers.\nThis model is particularly relevant as the market transitions from model development to production inference, where computing systems operate continuously to deliver AI outputs at scale.\nUnder this financing structure, AI cloud operators can procure Nvidia infrastructure with credit support, with revenue sharing linked to the cloud services sold based on that capacity.\nNvidia will receive its standard product revenue from infrastructure sales, in addition to a share of the cloud revenue generat…",
  "statement": "Nvidia has unveiled a revenue-sharing financing model to assist AI cloud providers in acquiring its computing infrastructure, thereby enhancing access to large-scale AI compute for startups, enterprises, researchers, and regional providers."
 },
 {
  "id": "U18",
  "article_id": "gnews:9e0d5b0d6b3e603c60340128",
  "title": "Nvidia Launches GPU Backstop Financing Model, Takes Cut of Cloud Revenue From Neocloud Partners",
  "publication_day": "2026-07-03",
  "article_opening": "Nvidia will act as a financial backstop for neocloud GPU deployments, agreeing to rent back unused GPUs at a fixed rate in exchange for a share of cloud revenue generated on that capacity [1]\nSharon AI plans to deploy up to 40,000 Grace Blackwell GB300 GPUs in Australia under a six-year agreement; Firmus Technologies targets up to 170,000 GPUs in a 360MW DSX-aligned facility in Batam, Indonesia [1] [3]\nThe revenue-share percentage was not disclosed; Nvidia declined to provide details beyond its blog post co-authored by CFO Colette Kress [3]\nThe structure gives Nvidia both standard hardware rev",
  "passage": "…sclosed, and Nvidia declined to offer further details beyond the blog post [3] .\nThe backstop mechanism works by Nvidia agreeing to rent back unused GPUs at a fixed rate, effectively de-risking lender exposure and making it easier for smaller cloud operators to secure capital for large GPU deployments [1] [3] . The structure echoes — but formalizes — earlier arrangements in which Nvidia provided demand guarantees to help neoclouds like CoreWeave and Lambda raise billions in debt financing [1] .\nUnder the AI Compute Partnership, Nvidia guarantees a floor utilization rate on deployed GPUs. If a neocloud partner's customer demand falls short, Nvidia commits to renting the idle capacity at a predetermined rate, absorbing the downside risk that would otherwise sit with lenders or equity investors [1] [3] . In exchange, Nvidia earns both its standard product revenue from the hardware sale and a recurring, usage-linked share of the cloud revenue generated on the supported capacity [1] .\nThe model addresses a persistent bottleneck in the neocloud sector: smaller operators often have customer demand in hand but cannot secure financing quickly enough to build infrastructure, because lenders view GPU residual values as uncertain [4] . By backstopping idle capacity, Nvidia effectively underwrites the as…",
  "statement": "Under the AI Compute Partnership, Nvidia guarantees a floor utilization rate on deployed GPUs. If a neocloud partner's customer demand falls short, Nvidia commits to renting the idle capacity at a predetermined rate, absorbing the downside risk that would otherwise sit with lenders or equity investors [1] [3] ."
 },
 {
  "id": "U19",
  "article_id": "gnews:552f8c2bdfd080555d592587",
  "title": "Nvidia's next-gen AI rack system delayed to 2028 on manufacturing snags, SemiAnalysis says",
  "publication_day": "2026-07-05",
  "article_opening": "Jensen Huang, chief executive officer of Nvidia Corp., speaks next to a Vera Rubin Ultra Kyber compute tray and a Vera Rubin Ultra Kyber NVLink midplane during a keynote address at the Nvidia GTC conference in San Jose, California, March 16, 2026.\nNvidia 's next marquee product — the Kyber rack-scale architecture designed to house its 2027 Rubin Ultra chips — has been delayed by more than 12 months to 2028, according to research firm SemiAnalysis, the latest in a string of reported setbacks raising questions about the AI giant's product roadmap.\nNvidia rejected the SemiAnalysis report and said",
  "passage": "…firm SemiAnalysis, the latest in a string of reported setbacks raising questions about the AI giant's product roadmap.\nNvidia rejected the SemiAnalysis report and said, \"Our roadmap is intact.\"\nShares of the artificial intelligence chip giant climbed about 1% on Monday.\nKyber is a server cabinet that packs 144 of Nvidia's most powerful chips into a single unit so they can work together as one giant computer, providing the horsepower AI companies need to train and run their most advanced models.\nThe design mounts graphics processing units in compute trays that sit vertically instead of horizontally to boost density and reduce latency, and had been slated to debut with Vera Rubin Ultra, Nvidia's next-generation rack-scale system, in 2027 .\nThe setback stems from difficulties manufacturing a key circuit board at the heart of the system, SemiAnalysis said in a post on Monday.\n\"Kyber NVL144 rack architecture has been delayed to 2028 as the PCB midplane remains challenging from a manufacturability standpoint,\" the firm said, referring to a specialized, multi-layer printed circuit board that connects electronic modules within a system.\nNVL576 — a larger system linking eight racks via optical connections — is also likely delayed or lim…",
  "statement": "The design mounts graphics processing units in compute trays that sit vertically instead of horizontally to boost density and reduce latency, and had been slated to debut with Vera Rubin Ultra, Nvidia's next-generation rack-scale system, in 2027 ."
 },
 {
