  "publication_day": "2026-07-03",
  "article_opening": "Nvidia will act as a financial backstop for neocloud GPU deployments, agreeing to rent back unused GPUs at a fixed rate in exchange for a share of cloud revenue generated on that capacity [1]\nSharon AI plans to deploy up to 40,000 Grace Blackwell GB300 GPUs in Australia under a six-year agreement; Firmus Technologies targets up to 170,000 GPUs in a 360MW DSX-aligned facility in Batam, Indonesia [1] [3]\nThe revenue-share percentage was not disclosed; Nvidia declined to provide details beyond its blog post co-authored by CFO Colette Kress [3]\nThe structure gives Nvidia both standard hardware rev",
  "passage": "… to 170,000 GPUs in a 360MW DSX-aligned facility in Batam, Indonesia [1] [3]\nThe revenue-share percentage was not disclosed; Nvidia declined to provide details beyond its blog post co-authored by CFO Colette Kress [3]\nThe structure gives Nvidia both standard hardware revenue and a recurring, usage-linked earnings stream — a shift from pure equipment sales toward platform-level economics [1]\nSHAZ shares fell 14.2% to $67.91 on July 3; NVDA dipped 1.4% to $194.83 on a $4.7 trillion market cap [5]\nNvidia on July 1 unveiled a new financing vehicle it calls the \"AI Compute Partnership,\" under which the chipmaker acts as a financial backstop for neocloud customers' GPU deployments and, in return, takes a recurring share of the cloud revenue those GPUs generate [1] . The program's first two adopters are Sharon AI (NASDAQ: SHAZ), an Australian sovereign-cloud provider planning up to 40,000 Grace Blackwell GB300 GPUs, and Firmus Technologies, which is building a 360MW campus in Batam, Indonesia, targeting up to 170,000 Nvidia GPUs [1] [2] .\nThe arrangement creates a dual revenue stream for Nvidia: the company collects standard product revenue from the GPU sale, then earns an additional usage-linked cut of the cloud services those chips power. Nvidia described…",
  "statement": "Nvidia on July 1 unveiled a new financing vehicle it calls the \"AI Compute Partnership,\" under which the chipmaker acts as a financial backstop for neocloud customers' GPU deployments and, in return, takes a recurring share of the cloud revenue those GPUs generate [1] ."
 },
 {
  "id": "U24",
  "article_id": "gnews:4d1677af37befd53ca3e7b45",
  "title": "NVIDIA is about to start taking a share of cloud providers' revenue.",
  "publication_day": "2026-07-01",
  "article_opening": "It has been reported that, in an effort to break the monopoly of tech giants, NVIDIA is providing financial guarantees for emerging cloud providers by offering GPU computing capacity lease-back arrangements to address their financing challenges. In exchange, NVIDIA will take a revenue share from these cloud providers, directly participating in profit distribution in the downstream computing market and extending its control further down the industrial chain.\nNVIDIA is leveraging its strong balance sheet to gain market leverage, quietly evolving from a chip vendor into the 'central bank' of the ",
  "passage": "…rtnership.' A NVIDIA spokesperson also confirmed the existence of the program. This move marks a significant strategic shift for NVIDIA:\nOn one hand, it lowers financing barriers for emerging cloud service providers to expand its customer base; on the other, it directly participates in profit distribution within the downstream computing market through revenue sharing, thereby extending its control further down the AI value chain.\nBusiness Model Shift: From Selling Chips to Sharing Cloud Revenue\nAccording to NVIDIA’s official press release, the company will earn not only standard product revenue but also an additional share of cloud service revenue, creating a usage-based, recurring revenue stream. The core objective of this model is to overcome the long-standing financing barrier that has constrained startups from accessing large-scale computing power.\nNVIDIA positions this framework as the 'DSX AI Factory' model, targeting AI service scenarios requiring continuous cross-regional operations, high utilization rates, and multi-tenant accelerated computing.\nSharon AI and Firmus are the first cloud providers participating in this model. Sharon AI plans to deploy up to 40,000 NVIDIA Grace Bla…",
