 {
  "id": "U56",
  "article_id": "gnews:c8c39f971721fd820aab5c1c",
  "title": "China to let AI firms buy Nvidia H200s, says report",
  "publication_day": "2026-07-08",
  "article_opening": "China to let AI firms buy Nvidia H200s, says report\nNvidia’s H200 hails from the company’s Hopper line of processors used for training and running AI models.\nChina will allow top AI companies like Alibaba and ByteDance to buy limited amounts of Nvidia's H200 AI chips, easing previous restrictions.\nChinese firms must request approval specifying the number of chips and reasons for purchase, with a possible total below 200,000.\nThe move responds to high AI demand but aims to protect China's chip industry and address cybersecurity concerns amid US-China tech tensions.\nChina plans to allow top arti",
  "passage": "…le AI chips to China on national security grounds.\nThe Chinese government, meanwhile, is still figuring out how many H200 chips to allow the companies to buy, but the total may end up being fewer than 200,000, the Information reported. When it comes to AI infrastructure, that is not a large number. A single data centre can use more than 400,000 Blackwell processors.\nIn May, Nvidia chief financial officer Colette Kress said the H200 chips had yet to generate any revenue for the company in China.\n“We are uncertain whether any imports will be allowed into the country,” she said.",
  "statement": "“We are uncertain whether any imports will be allowed into the country,” she said."
 },
