 U17 FULL FIRST: Nvidia introduces revenue-sharing model for AI cloud financing
Nvidia has launched a revenue-sharing financing model aimed at AI cloud providers, facilitating access to its computing infrastructure. This initiative is designed to support startups and enterprises in scaling AI operations.
Nvidia launches a revenue-sharing financing model for AI cloud providers.
The model aims to facilitate access to large-scale AI compute for startups and enterprises.
Sharon AI and Firmus are early adopters of the financing structure.
The initiative reflects a shift in the AI market towards production inference.
Nvidia has unveiled a revenue-sharing financing model to assist AI cloud providers in acquiring its computing infrastructure, thereby enhancing access to large-scale AI compute for startups, enterprises, researchers, and regional providers.
This model is particularly relevant as the market transitions from model development to production inference, where computing systems operate continuously to deliver AI outputs at scale.
Under this financing structure, AI cloud operators can procure Nvidia infrastructure with credit support, with revenue sharing linked to the cloud services sold based on that capacity.
Nvidia will receive its standard product revenue from infrastructure sales, in addition to a share of the cloud revenue generated from the supported capacity. This approach offers AI cloud operators a viable means to finance large installations, which have traditionally been challenging to fund, especially for emerging companies lacking the balance sheet strength typically required for capital-intensive projects.
The initiative reflects a significant shift in the AI market, where demand is increasingly focused not only on training large models but also on running them in production for enterprises, software providers, and AI-native businesses that require constant access to computing resources for inference, fine-tuning, and related workloads.
Two companies are among the first to collaborate with Nvidia under this model. Sharon AI plans to deploy up to 40,000 Nvidia Grace Blackwell GB300 GPUs, while Firmus is developing a DSX AI factory campus in Batam, Indonesia, which is expected to scale to 360 megawatts and accommodate up to 170,000 Nvidia GPUs. These projects exemplify the scale of infrastructure Nvidia aims to facilitate through this financing model, which is linked to future usage rather than relying solely on conventional long-term commitments.
